Feature: Lease teardown with guest sessions and remote sessions

  @AC-12 @TL-2 @TL-4
  Scenario: Release cancels a command running over a guest session
    Given a running lease on guest link "ssh-session"
    And an exec of "sleep 600" running
    When the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then the exec fails with "command cancelled by release"
    And no "sleep 600" process remains in the guest or on the host

  @AC-13 @TL-2 @TL-4
  Scenario: Release and destroy leave no guest session behind
    Given a lease with an open guest session
    When the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then the session's channel process has exited
    And no guest session agent process remains for that lease

  @AC-14 @TL-2
  Scenario: A daemon restart makes in-flight calls uncertain, never repeated
    Given a client with a remote session and an exec in flight
    When vm-service restarts
    Then the client's resume is answered "expired"
    And a query of the exec's execution ID answers "unknown"
    And the client reports the exec as uncertain without sending it again

Feature: Hygiene and coexistence on durable machines

  @AC-30 @TL-2 @TL-6
  Scenario: Release sweeps what the lease left running, and nothing else
    Given a durable lease
    And a stand-in CI runner process tree that started during the lease
    When the agent starts a background process with "nohup sleep 3600 &"
    And the agent opens an application with "open -a TextEdit"
    And the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then the sleep process and TextEdit have ended
    And the stand-in CI processes, Consul and the registrar are still running
    And the hygiene record lists each swept process with the rule that matched it
    And the workspace has been removed and the machine is "available"

  @AC-31 @TL-2 @TL-6
  Scenario: The inventory before and after is recorded and never refuses
    Given a durable lease
    When the agent installs a Homebrew formula and writes a file into "~/Library/Application Support"
    And the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then changes.json lists the formula as added and the Application Support entry as added or changed
    And the release response carries changes.json and the paths of both inventories
    When one inventory part times out at release
    Then the part is recorded as missing with its reason, the release still succeeds and the machine is "available"

  @AC-32 @TL-6
  Scenario: A lease coexists with the CI runner
    Given a Mac whose CI runner is running a job
    When the orchestrator acquires its durable machine
    Then the acquisition succeeds and the CI runner is neither paused nor stopped
    And the machine record reports ci_runner busy
    When the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then the hygiene record lists the intervals during which a CI job ran
    And nothing under "~/actions-runner" was changed by the release

  @AC-33 @TL-2
  Scenario: A service restart keeps the durable lease
    Given a durable lease with a background process the lease started
    When machine-service restarts
    Then the lease is still "leased" with the same lease_id
    And the background process is still running
    When the orchestrator releases the lease
    Then the background process ends and the hygiene record is complete
